A self-hosted Mica answers two public endpoints. Neither one needs a sign-in, and neither one names the repository, the branch or the uptime. The local editor answers both; its `/ready` has no `reasons`.

## /health

```bash
curl -s https://cms.example.com/health
```

```json
{ "status": "ok" }
```

`/health` checks only that the process answers. It does no input or output, so a problem at the Git host does not restart the container. Use it as the liveness check of your platform, for example the Docker `HEALTHCHECK` (the image has one).

## /ready

```bash
curl -s https://cms.example.com/ready
```

```json
{ "status": "ok", "repository": "ok", "reasons": [], "lastSyncAt": "2026-10-14T10:00:00.000Z" }
```

`/ready` tells whether editors can work. It answers with HTTP 200 when `status` is `ok` or `degraded`, and with 503 when it is `error`. Mica keeps a result for 30 seconds, so many requests do not cause many checks.

| Key | Values | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `status` | `ok`, `degraded`, `error` | `ok`: editors can save and publish. `degraded`: editors can save, but something needs a person, such as a Publish that cannot work. `error`: editors cannot work. |
| `repository` | `ok`, `degraded`, `unavailable` | The state of the clone. |
| `reasons` | a list of codes | Why somebody should look. |
| `lastSyncAt` | a time, or `null` | When a sync last reached the Git host. |

The reason codes. Each code makes the status `degraded`, except `cloning` (`error`) and `conflict` (no change):

| Code | Meaning | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `cloning` | The first clone runs. `/ready` answers 503 until it is done. | Wait. |
| `auth_failed` | The Git host refused the token. | Renew the token. |
| `write_forbidden` | The token cannot push. | Give the token write access, or check branch protection. |
| `repo_not_found` | The Git host does not know the repository, or the token cannot see it. | Correct `MICA_REPOSITORY_URL`, or the token's access. |
| `branch_not_found` | The branch does not exist at the Git host. | Create the branch, or correct `MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH`. |
| `network` | The Git host cannot be reached. | Check DNS and outgoing HTTPS from the container. Mica tries again at each sync. |
| `diverged` | The branch at the Git host does not contain Mica's last commit, for example after a force push. | Read the log. See "Troubleshooting" in [Self-hosting](/docs/self-hosting/). |
| `prefetch_incomplete` | A sync could not fetch all files of the new version. | Mica tries again at each sync. If it stays, read the log. |
| `fast_forward_failed` | A sync could not bring the clone to the new version. | Read the log. |
| `sync_failing` | Another sync problem. | Read the log, and run `docker compose run --rm mica check`. |
| `pending_push` | A publication is committed in the clone, but the Git host did not take it yet. | Mica tries again at each sync. Look at the other codes for the cause. |
| `restart_required` | `.gitattributes` changed at the Git host. Mica keeps the old version of the branch until a restart checks it. | Restart the container. |
| `read_only` | A sync failed while it changed the files, and Mica could not undo it. Editors cannot save or publish. | Read the log, then restart the container: the start finishes the sync. |
| `conflict` | An editor's draft and a change at the Git host touch the same entry. | Nothing: the editor chooses a version in the editor. |

While a sync fails, editors can still edit and save: their work is in the clone. Publish works again when the Git host accepts the push.

Use `/ready` for a readiness check or for monitoring, not for a restart. See [Self-hosting](/docs/self-hosting/) and [Command line](/docs/reference-cli/).

## What to alert on

- Alert when `status` is not `ok` for longer than a few sync intervals. A short `network` problem is normal.
- Alert at once on `auth_failed`, `write_forbidden` and `read_only`: they do not go away without a person.
- Alert when `lastSyncAt` is older than one hour.
- Do not alert on `conflict`.
